@page "/usage"

<PageTitle>BlazorDashboardKit — usage</PageTitle>

<h1>Usage</h1>

<section>
    <h2>1. Install &amp; register</h2>
    <pre><code>dotnet add package BlazorDashboardKit</code></pre>
    <p>In <code>Program.cs</code> (every render target — server <em>and</em> <code>.Client</code> for a Web App):</p>
    <pre><code>builder.Services.AddBlazorDashboard()
    .AddDashboardWidget&lt;CounterWidget&gt;(CounterWidget.Descriptor)
    .AddDashboardWidget&lt;MarkdownNoteWidget&gt;(MarkdownNoteWidget.Descriptor);</code></pre>
    <p>
        No manual asset tags: the kit's Blazor JS initializer injects its CSS and
        GridStack automatically. Persist with
        <code>AddBlazorDashboard(o =&gt; o.UseJsonFileStore("/data"))</code> or a
        custom <code>IDashboardStore</code>.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>2. Drop in the host</h2>
    <pre><code>@@using BlazorDashboardKit.Components

&lt;DashboardHost OwnerKey="@@userId" EditMode="true" /&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
        <code>ReadOnly="true"</code> hides edit affordances (use it for static SSR).
        The host is render-mode-safe: it degrades to a readable stacked layout
        until JavaScript is available.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>3. Write a widget</h2>
    <p>Derive <code>BaseWidgetComponent&lt;TConfig&gt;</code> and expose a static <code>Descriptor</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>@@inherits BaseWidgetComponent&lt;MyWidget.MyConfig&gt;

&lt;div&gt;@@(TypedConfig?.Title)&lt;/div&gt;

@@code {
    public class MyConfig { public string Title { get; set; } = "Hi"; }

    public static readonly WidgetDescriptor Descriptor = new() {
        Type = "My", Name = "My Widget", Category = "Demo",
        ConfigComponentType = typeof(MyConfigEditor)
    };
}</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>4. Give it a config component</h2>
    <p>
        A widget's settings UI is a real Blazor component deriving
        <code>WidgetConfigComponent&lt;TConfig&gt;</code> — full control, any inputs,
        validation, anything. Bind to <code>Model</code> and call
        <code>NotifyChangedAsync()</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>@@inherits WidgetConfigComponent&lt;MyWidget.MyConfig&gt;

&lt;input value="@@Model.Title" @@onchange="OnTitle" /&gt;

@@code {
    async Task OnTitle(ChangeEventArgs e) {
        Model.Title = e.Value?.ToString() ?? "";
        await NotifyChangedAsync();
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>The panel owns Save/Cancel — <code>Model</code> is an isolated working copy.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Draggable / resizable GridStack grid; per-widget min/max size from the descriptor.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple dashboards</strong> per owner — switch, add, delete (last one protected).</li>
        <li><strong>Lock</strong> a widget to pin it; <strong>duplicate</strong> a widget (independent config).</li>
        <li>Two-step confirm on destructive actions (remove widget, delete dashboard).</li>
        <li>Per-widget component-based configuration; export / import the layout as JSON.</li>
        <li>Render-mode-safe: Blazor Server, WebAssembly, and static SSR (graceful CSS fallback, read-only).</li>
        <li>Zero-config assets (Blazor JS initializer); themeable via <code>--bdk-*</code> tokens with automatic dark mode.</li>
        <li>Keyboard/pointer dismissable dropdowns, ARIA labels, no Bootstrap-JS dependency.</li>
        <li><code>OnDashboardChanged</code> hook and tunable <code>GridOptions</code> for host integration.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

